  "observations": [
    "Compared with 3600s at RAW UUID, 900s namespace p50 37.270->48.052ms (+28.93%), CPU 61.184->88.962ms (+45.40%), read_rows 102368->218592 (+113.5%), control drift 0.142%. Strong within-run result.",
    "Compared with 3600s at RAW UUID, 10800s reduces disk from 7424806 to 6833238 B (-7.97%) and read_rows for node broad 57728->38592; node_broad p50 regresses 91.906->95.784ms (+4.22%) while namespace p50 37.526->36.709ms (-2.18%, comparable to control drift1.72%). No global 10800s winner.",
    "RAW UUID is ~2%-7% faster than LC across step sizes; relative gains smaller than original sparse-Jenkins high-churn fixture. 900s has more ID rows and more read/decode work.",
    "Both 900s and 10800s changes affect data layout; their write/merge CPU and storage cost must be included before promotion."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Synthetic fixed 3h life and one-query-client normal run, not production HPA/ACL/staleness/concurrent tenant traffic.",
    "Only 1d avg_over_time lookback queries and 3d daily steps; cannot infer behavior for 15m or 1h lookback and true fine time pruning.",
    "Same runner long-lived server and warm caches; use exact same-runner matched paired comparisons only, not unrelated runs.",
    "glibc-linked O1 lab binary; jemalloc production parity not established.",
    "Representative CPU traces are diagnostic samples, not critical path timing; sum of processor wait times is not wall time."
  ],
  "next_action": "Same raw UUID and fixture, test 15m, 1h and 3h lookback at within-bucket and bucket-boundary evaluations (900/3600/10800) using A/B/B/A, prove time-pruning/value parity and read_rows tradeoff. Do not change global bucket default based on 1d results alone."
